{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>After YHWH, etc.</b> It is difficult: why did He interrupt in the middle of this verse with the laws of the false prophet, since afterward He says ״והנביא ההוא וגו׳ יומת״ — He should have said it after the verse ״והנביא״ etc. Further, one needs to know why He lengthened all these expressions: <b>תלכו, תיראו, תשמרו, תשמעו, תעבדו, תדבקון</b>?" "<b>And it appears</b> that He comes to alert to the general principles that exist in the service of YHWH, so that you may learn from them to render the prophet and the dreamer liable when he says to perform any one of them for idolatry. And He began by saying ״אחרי ה׳ אלהיכם תלכו״ — this is the commandment of accepting the yoke of the kingdom of heaven, to walk after YHWH and not after any other. From this you learn that if the prophet says to accept idolatry upon themselves, to walk after it — he is liable. And He was precise in saying ״תלכו״ in the future tense, to alert you regarding the measure of the prophet's liability: that even if he did not say ״arise and worship״ now, but rather ״accept idolatry upon yourselves as a god for tomorrow״ — he is liable to death from the time he said these things to them, even though his words are for the future." "And His saying <b>and Him you shall fear</b> — meaning, specifically Him you shall fear and not idolatry. The intention is that if he comes to say: fear such-and-such idolatry, even though he did not say to do anything for it nor to serve it but only to fear it, lest it harm him — and this does not diminish his fear of the Blessed One, even so he is liable to death, for Him alone shall they fear." "And His saying <b>and et His commandments you shall keep</b> — His commandments and not the commandments of idolatry. The intention in this is that if idolatry commands to do something in which there is enjoyment, such as if idolatry says: ״whoever wishes to profit should do this thing and profit״ and the like, and there is in this act nothing that touches upon idolatry, and there is room to say: since there is in this thing no species of worship at all, there is nothing in it — a teaching comes to say: His commandments, may He be blessed, you shall keep. And if the prophet comes to say that HaShem commanded him that they should do things of idolatry in this manner and succeed — he shall be put to death." "And His saying <b>and to His voice you shall listen</b>, meaning, specifically to His voice, may He be blessed, is what you must listen to, to learn and to teach His pure utterances, and not to the voice of idolatry, for if idolatry uttered certain sayings it is forbidden to hear them. And if the prophet comes and says that HaShem commanded to go to idolatry to hear its words, even though they contain neither an act nor fear of it, even so he shall be put to death." "And the verse's saying <b>and Him you shall serve</b> — meaning, to HaShem and not to any other. And the intention behind this is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Gittin 57a), that a Caesar said to a small child of Hannah that he should contrive to throw his ring from his hand before an idol and go and bring it and show to onlookers that he had worshipped, even though he has no intention in his heart to worship it and did not wish to, etc. Therefore the verse comes here to say that if a prophet comes to speak in the name of HaShem as though HaShem permits him to do so, that prophet shall be put to death." "And the verse's saying <b>and in Him you shall cleave</b> is explained along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Ketubot 111b): Is it possible to cleave to the Shekhinah? Rather, one cleaves to those who learn Torah, for they are a chariot for the Shekhinah, and it is as though he cleaved to Him, blessed be He. And here HaShem commanded that one should not cleave except to the intimates of HaShem and His holy ones, and the verse comes to exclude — that one should not cleave to the intimates of idolatry; and if a prophet comes and says in the name of HaShem to do so, that prophet shall be put to death.\nOr perhaps this is what the verse intended in saying <b>and the sign or portent comes, saying: Let us go after other elohim</b> — and it did not say ״let us go and serve other elohim,״ to say that they are liable even if they did not say to serve. And even though it concludes by saying ״and serve them,״ the intention in this is that in the end he says ״and serve them,״ and in any case one is liable for every particular that HaShem traced in the verse ״after HaShem״ etc. And therefore it concludes by saying <b>and that prophet</b> etc. — meaning, one who wished to contradict what was stated shall be put to death.\nAnd the reason for the death penalty the verse states: <b>for he spoke rebellion against HaShem</b> — and even though he did not explicitly incite to idolatrous worship, nevertheless the verse makes him liable because he lied about HaShem, that he prophesied such evil things. And HaShem makes known that prophecy of this kind His prophets do not prophesy. And even though He may have him prophesy to tell Israel to nullify some commandment as a temporary decree, that is specifically regarding other commandments; but regarding idolatry in any form of prohibition — whether a matter for which one is liable to death or a matter for which one is not liable to death — HaShem will not agree to it being done even temporarily. And if a prophet says so, he has lied in the name of HaShem. And so they said in the Sifrei, and this is their language: ״for he spoke rebellion״ — that he came to falsify the words of the Holy One, blessed be He, end quote. And for his falsification he is put to death." "<b>And besides</b> our approach, the verse may be explained as coming to warn concerning the totality of the Torah, which it alluded to by way of the six Orders of the Mishnah in this manner: <b>after HaShem</b> etc. <b>you shall walk</b> — this is the Order of Zera'im. For since they contain the laws of gleanings, forgotten sheaves, the corner of the field, and gifts for the poor, and all of these are among His attributes, blessed be He, and also regarding the law of second tithe, which one must bring to the place that HaShem will choose, and also regarding the law of the Levitical tithe — meaning, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 31): There was an incident with a certain man who used to give his tithes, and his field would produce for him a thousandfold, etc. After some time that man died, and his son arose; in the first year he begrudged his tithe and diminished below the required amount. The following year his field produced for him only the same amount as he had given the previous year. The next year he diminished still further, and his field produced for him the same amount as he had given the year before, and so on in this manner, end quote. The matter proves that according to the amount he would give, so would his field produce for him. Therefore the statement of the Blessed One comes here and commands him that he should walk after the deeds of the Blessed One — to give according to what He gives him — and he should not cause HaShem to walk after him according to what he gives of his tithes, in the manner we have written." "<b>Further,</b> the verse intended to use this expression corresponding to the Order of Zera'im, because in it lies the sustenance of man and his food. And the Rambam wrote in Hilkhot De'ot (ch. 3, hal. 2) that a person must intend in his eating and drinking to heal his body for the service of HaShem, etc., as it is written (Proverbs 3:6) ״בְּכָל דְּרָכֶיךָ דָעֵהוּ״ etc., and that is the meaning of <b>after HaShem your Elohim you shall walk</b> — in your ways." "<b>And Him you shall fear</b> — this is the Order of Mo'ed. And they alluded to this in the word otto (אֹתוֹ), for the Sabbath is called an ot (sign), as it is written (Exodus 31:17) ״אוֹת הִיא״ (it is a sign). Also the Festivals are called otot (signs), and the verse attributes to them fear, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory, in Sefer Tikkunei ha-Zohar (Tikkun 9): ״one who fears the Sabbath״ — that one must fear their punishment." "<b>And you shall keep His commandments</b> — this is the Order of Women, for one must keep many commandments in the laws of women and the prohibitions of relations and bonds." "<b>And to His voice you shall listen</b> — this is the Order of Damages, and the phrase <b>and to His voice you shall listen</b> alludes that one must incline the ear to whatever the judges rule to obligate the liable party and to acquit the innocent party, and one must not deviate from anything they instruct him, as Scripture says (Deuteronomy 17:12): ״וְהָאִישׁ … לְבִלְתִּי שְׁמֹעַ אֶל הַכֹּהֵן … אוֹ אֶל הַשּׁוֹפֵט״ etc." "<b>And Him shall you serve</b> — this is the Order of Holy Things, for it is entirely the laws of the service of the House of our Elohim." "<b>And in Him shall you cleave</b> — this is the Order of Purities, which is entirely the laws of purities, and he alluded in the phrase <b>and in Him shall you cleave</b> that through the medium of purity one becomes worthy to cleave to the Shekhinah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:35.837762313Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}